parsed_output.summary:
Root-caused the CWE-079 CSS-to-output XSS: CssValidator.lexicalValueToString() echoed the decoded content of CSS string/identifier/URI tokens (from font-family names, generic idents, and url() values) verbatim into the reconstructed 'clean' CSS, with no check for HTML metacharacters. When such a token contains '<'/'>' and a policy's value regex is permissive enough to allow it through (as bundled/custom policies can be, e.g. font-family's regex intentionally allows quotes/letters), those raw angle brackets survive into the sanitized output. For the <style>-tag path specifically, this is directly exploitable: I proved with a regression test that pre-patch, a CSS string value containing a literal '<' character lands unescaped inside the <style> element's raw-text content ('<style><![CDATA[p {
	font-family: x<y;
}
]]></style>'), which lets an attacker smuggle a premature '</style>' close plus new markup (e.g. <script>) past the sanitizer -- exactly the 'STYLE tag smuggling' the alert describes. Verified the style-ATTRIBUTE path is already safe because the downstream DOM/SAX HTML serializer correctly escapes '<'/'>'/'"' in attribute values, but the <style>-element text-node path is not escaped (correctly, per HTML5 raw-text rules) which is why the character must never reach that buffer unescaped in the first place.
